---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import SplitPdfPagesInHalf from '@/components/tools/pdf/SplitPdfPagesInHalf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="split-pdf-pages-in-half">
  <SplitPdfPagesInHalf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Why split PDF pages in half</h2>
      <p>
        When you scan an open book on a flatbed scanner, each scan captures two printed pages. A two-up
        conference handout has two slides on each sheet. In both cases the PDF has half the pages it should,
        each twice as wide, and it is hard to read on a phone, an e-reader or a tablet in portrait.
      </p>
      <p>
        Split each sheet down the middle, and a 180-page spread scan becomes a 360-page document that fits any
        screen and prints one page to a sheet.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Finding the spine automatically</h2>
      <p>
        Press <strong>Find the spine</strong> and this tab renders the page in the preview at low resolution.
        The tool counts the dark pixels in each pixel column, which gives an ink-density profile across the
        width of the page. The gutter between two printed pages is the emptiest column near the middle, so
        the cut goes at the lowest point of that profile inside the central third.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the profile has no clear minimum, as with a full-bleed photo or a table across the spread, the
        tool tells you, and you place the line by hand.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Gutter trim for a dark inner edge</h2>
      <p>
        A book pressed onto a scanner glass curves away near the spine. The curve shows as a grey-to-black
        band a few millimetres wide down the inner edge of both halves. After splitting, the band is at the
        edge of a page, where it is more visible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Set <strong>Trim at the cut</strong> to 3–8 mm to remove that band from both halves. The trim is
        symmetrical around the split line, so the two pages keep the same width. Start small: too much trim
        cuts into the inner margin of the text.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Reading order</h2>
      <p>
        For Latin-script books the left half of a spread comes first. For Arabic, Hebrew, Japanese manga and
        traditional Chinese, the right half comes first. If you switch the reading order, the tool writes the
        two halves in the opposite sequence. The cut stays the same, so you can change this later without a
        new cut.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same setting works for a top-and-bottom split: you choose whether the top or the bottom half
        comes first, which matters for two-up handouts printed in a column.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why the file does not double in size</h2>
      <p>
        The tool makes each half by embedding the source page with a clipping box, without re-rendering it.
        Both halves point at the same scan image inside the PDF; only the clipping instructions are new. A
        400 MB book scan becomes a document with twice the page count, about the same file size and exactly
        the original resolution.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pages with a rotation flag also work: the tool maps the cut you draw on screen back through the
        rotation, and the halves come out the right way up.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Workflow for a whole book</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Split the spreads here. Leave &ldquo;only split wide pages&rdquo; on so the cover stays whole.</li>
        <li>Check the first and last spread, because scanners often shift alignment over a long run.</li>
        <li>If some pages are upside down, fix them with <a href="/rotate-pdf">Rotate PDF</a>.</li>
        <li>If the scan captured the scanner glass edges, trim the outer edges with <a href="/crop-pdf">Crop PDF</a>.</li>
        <li>Make the file smaller for a reader with <a href="/compress-pdf">Compress PDF</a>.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
